meta {
  name: Follow a short code - from cache
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/{{linkCode}}
  body: none
  auth: none
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: true
  followRedirects: false
}

tests {
  test("answers exactly what the first request answered", function () {
    // The point of the assertion is that it is the *same*: from T050 this
    // response comes out of Redis and the previous one came out of Postgres,
    // and a visitor must not be able to tell which.
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(302);
    expect(res.getHeader("location")).to.equal("https://example.com/Hello?utm_source=poster");
    expect(res.getHeader("cache-control")).to.equal("no-store");
  });

  test("still sets no cookie (FR-015, Principle V)", function () {
    expect(res.getHeader("set-cookie")).to.be.undefined;
  });

  test("still sends an empty body", function () {
    expect(res.body).to.be.empty;
  });
}

docs {
  The same request as the one before it, and that is the whole of the test.

  From Phase 3C the first request for a code is a cache miss — one Postgres read
  through `RedirectMiddleware`, which then writes `link:<code>` to Redis — and
  every request after it is a cache hit answered by a single `GETEX` with no
  Postgres access at all (research.md D1, D6). Those are two different code
  paths producing what must be one response.

  This collection cannot see which path answered; `spec/middleware/redirect_middleware_spec.rb`
  asserts the absence of the query directly. What it can see, and what a client
  actually depends on, is that the status, the `Location`, the `Cache-Control`
  and the absence of a cookie do not change between them.

  ## What changed under this in 3C

  The click is no longer written during the request. The middleware pushes
  `<link_id>:<timestamp>` onto the `clicks:buffer` Redis list after the response
  triple is built, and `Clicks::FlushJob` drains it into Postgres every five
  seconds in batches of a thousand (D4, FR-020, FR-021). So a counter read
  immediately after this request may still be one behind; SC-005 gives it thirty
  seconds, and the flush schedule spends five of them.

  A Redis failure at that point is not visible here either, and must not be:
  the response is constructed before the click is recorded, so losing a click
  costs statistics and never a redirect (FR-022, SC-008).

  Requires `linkCode`, set by `02 - Links / Create link`, and is only meaningful
  when it runs after `Follow a short code`.
}
